`static void *default_malloc(size_t,void*)` :52-56 (ignores data, `malloc`); `static void default_free(void*,void*)` :59-63.\n- `void *_pcre2_memctl_malloc(size_t size, pcre2_memctl *memctl)` :81-97. `memctl==NULL` → raw `malloc(size)` and the new block's leading `pcre2_memctl` is filled with `{default_malloc, default_free, NULL}`; else `memctl->malloc(size, memctl->memory_data)` and the memctl struct is copied verbatim into the head of the block. Returns NULL on failure. The block therefore always begins with a `pcre2_memctl` (3 words: malloc fn, free fn, memory_data — 24 bytes LP64) used later by `*_free`.\n- `pcre2_memctl` layout is the first member of every real context/code/serialized block, which is why `(pcre2_memctl *)gcontext` casts work.\n\n## Create / copy / free\n| fn | line | notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `pcre2_general_context *pcre2_general_context_create(void *(*private_malloc)(size_t,void*), void (*private_free)(void*,void*), void *memory_data)` | :110-123 | NULL malloc → `default_malloc`; NULL free → `default_free`; allocates `sizeof(pcre2_real_general_context)` **using the supplied malloc** (so a custom malloc is called even for its own block); stores all three into `gcontext->memctl`. Returns NULL if the malloc returns NULL. Both NULL == identical behaviour to library default. |\n| `pcre2_compile_context *pcre2_compile_context_create(pcre2_general_context *gcontext)` | :147-157 | `memctl_malloc(sizeof(pcre2_real_compile_context), (pcre2_memctl*)gcontext)`, then `*ccontext = PRIV(default_compile_context)`, then if gcontext!=NULL re-overwrites the leading memctl from gcontext. gcontext==NULL → default malloc/free. |\n| `pcre2_match_context_create` | :183-193 | same pattern with `PRIV(default_match_context)` |\n| `pcre2_convert_context_create` | :213-223 | same pattern with `PRIV(default_convert_context)` |\n| `pcre2_general_context_copy` | :230-239 | `gcontext->memctl.malloc` + `memcpy` whole struct. **NULL arg → immediate deref/crash.** |\n| `pcre2_co