ternal carries its verbatim C signature and its reachability precondition (which need a populated `compile_block`, which need a compiled-code pointer plus `char_lists_end`, which are pure).\n\nThree findings worth flagging, as they are places where a differential harness would otherwise assert the wrong thing:\n\n1. `PCRE2_CONFIG_COMPILED_WIDTHS` returns **0**, not 1. The CMake build defines only `PCRE2_CODE_UNIT_WIDTH=8`, never `SUPPORT_PCRE2_8`, which is the macro `pcre2_config.c` actually tests.\n2. `_pcre2_ckd_smul` **never reports overflow** on LP64 here — `HAVE_BUILTIN_MUL_OVERFLOW` is undefined, so the guard short-circuits on `sizeof(m) > sizeof(*r)`. It also asserts non-negative inputs. A port using Rust's checked arithmetic would diverge, so row 119 pins the current behaviour rather than the intended semantics.\n3. `_pcre2_find_bracket`'s `number < 0` mode (find first `OP_REVERSE`/`OP_VREVERSE`) has **no in-tree caller** in 10.48 and is reachable only by direct call — row 275 targets it explicitly, since a port could silently drop it with no test failing.\n\nI also cross-checked the two data-table defects recorded in the existing `SYMBOLS.md` (`_pcre2_ucp_gentype_8` needing 30 elements, `_pcre2_ucd_nocase_ranges_8` needing 84 u32) against the C consumers, and both are genuinely dereferenced at those indices — `ucp_gentype[ucp_Zs]` at index 29 and `ranges + size` as a terminator. Row 290 asserts byte-for-byte parity on all exported data objects to keep that class of defect caught.","usage":{"total_tokens":288304,"tool_uses":45,"duration_ms":1742243},"uuid":"0760cca9-d32a-4c97-a922-2db55684e014","session_id":"aeab5d90-512d-4436-80fd-a67670a151c9"}
