{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_xpgmbnvex64yzy64kzu3dss6dhexzuuq23trwtn3r4iwlewaenkq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_013NSMVc8SARPYNsPV2hp5jr","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Write lowlevel internals diff test","subagent_type":"general-purpose","run_in_background":false,"prompt":"Write ONE new differential test file at\n`/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-GdDnk6/translated_rust/tests/lowlevel.rs`.\n\nRULES\n* Do NOT modify anything under `c_src/` (C is ground truth), do NOT modify `src/*.rs`,\n  do NOT modify `tests/common/*`, do NOT touch `SYMBOLS.md`/`ERRORS.md`/`CONFIGS.md`.\n  Put any helper you need inside your own test file.\n* The existing harness `tests/common/mod.rs` (READ IT FIRST) loads BOTH shared\n  libraries with `libloading` and exposes typed function pointers.  Use\n  `mod common; use common::*;` then `let p = pair();` → `p.c` is the C library's\n  `Api`, `p.r` the Rust library's.  Never call Rust functions directly — always via\n  `p.r.<field>`.  `common::consts::*` has every `PCRE2_*` constant.\n  `Rng::new(seed)` is a deterministic PRNG (`below(n)`, `byte()`, `bool_()`, `pick(&[..])`).\n* Build/run with:\n    cd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-GdDnk6/translated_rust\n    cargo build --release && cargo test --release --test lowlevel\n  (the Rust `.so` is dlopened, so `cargo build --release` MUST be run first).\n* The C source you are differentially testing is in `c_src/src/`; read the relevant\n  function to know exactly what the arguments and return values mean, especially\n  which inputs are legal (do NOT construct undefined-behaviour calls: e.g. do not\n  pass invalid UTF-8 with a \"no check\" flag, do not read uninitialised outputs).\n\nTASK — one `#[test]` per function group, each comparing C vs Rust over MANY\nrandomized inputs (fixed seed) plus hand-picked boundary values.  Cover ALL of these\nexported low-level symbols, whose signatures are already in `tests/common/mod.rs`:\n\n1. `_pcre2_strlen_8`, `_pcre2_strcmp_8`, `_pcre2_strcmp_c8_8`, `_pcre2_strncmp_8`,\n   `_pcre2_strncmp_c8_8`, `_pcre2_strcpy_c8_8` (`pcre2_string_utils.c`).\n   Random NUL-terminated buffers, equal/prefix/differing strings, empty strings,\n   high bytes (0x80..0xff), n = 0 / shorter / longer than both strings.  For\n   `strcpy_c8` check the copied bytes AND the returned length.\n   IMPORTANT: compare the *sign* semantics exactly as the C returns them.\n2. `_pcre2_ckd_smul_8` (`pcre2_chkdint.c`): read the C first — it asserts\n   non-negative inputs and, because `HAVE_BUILTIN_MUL_OVERFLOW` is NOT defined in\n   this build, the guard short-circuits on `sizeof`. Test the values the C actually\n   accepts (a, b >= 0), including 0, 1, large-but-non-overflowing, and the exact\n   overflow boundary the C code checks; compare both the return value and `*r`.\n3. `_pcre2_ord2utf_8`: every code point 0, 0x7f, 0x80, 0x7ff, 0x800, 0xffff,\n   0x10000, 0x10ffff, plus surrogates 0xd800..0xdfff and randoms; compare the\n   returned length and all written bytes.\n4. `_pcre2_valid_utf_8`: valid sequences of all lengths, plus every distinct\n   malformed case the C distinguishes (truncated at end, 0x80..0xbf as a leading\n   byte, 0xfe/0xff, overlong 2/3/4-byte forms, surrogate encodings, > 0x10ffff,\n   5- and 6-byte forms, bad continuation bytes at each position).  Compare the\n   returned error code AND `*erroroffset`.  Derive the cases from\n   `c_src/src/pcre2_valid_utf.c` — one per `PCRE2_ERROR_UTF8_ERRn`.\n5. `_pcre2_is_newline_8` and `_pcre2_was_newline_8`: all newline types\n   (`NLTYPE_FIXED`? read `pcre2_internal.h` for the `NLTYPE_*` values and how the\n   `type` argument encodes ANY/ANYCRLF/FIXED), utf = TRUE and FALSE, subjects\n   containing CR, LF, CRLF, VT, FF, NEL(0x85 and 0xc2 0x85), LS/PS\n   (0xe2 0x80 0xa8 / 0xa9), NUL, plain letters, and a pointer at the very\n   start/end of the buffer.  Compare the return value and `*lenptr`.\n6. `_pcre2_extuni_8(c, eptr, start_subject, end_subject, utf, xcount)`: extended\n   grapheme cluster stepping.  Use UTF-8 subjects with combining marks, regional\n   indicators, Hangul jamo, ZWJ emoji sequences, plus ASCII; utf TRUE and FALSE;\n   `xcount` NULL and non-NULL (compare the counted value too).  Compare the\n   returned pointer as an OFFSET from the subject start (never as an absolute\n   address).\n7. `_pcre2_script_run_8(ptr, endptr, utf)`: single-script runs, mixed-script runs,\n   digits from different scripts, empty and single-character ranges; utf TRUE/FALSE.\n8. `_pcre2_compile_get_hash_from_name8(name, len)`: random names of length 0..8.\n9. `_pcre2_memctl_malloc_8(size, memctl)`: build a `#[repr(C)]` struct mirroring\n   `pcre2_memctl` (`void *(*malloc)(size_t, void*); void (*free)(void*, void*); void *memory_data;`)\n   with your own Rust `extern \"C\"` malloc/free wrappers over libc, call it in both\n   libraries, verify both return a block whose embedded `pcre2_memctl` copy matches\n   what you passed (read `_pcre2_memctl_malloc` in `pcre2_context.c` to see what it\n   stores), then free it with your own free.  Also test the size-0 and\n   allocation-failure (malloc returns NULL) paths.\n10. `_pcre2_find_bracket_8(code, utf, number)`: you CAN reach this from outside.\n    Compile a pattern in each library, then compute `codestart` as\n    `PCRE2_INFO_NAMETABLE pointer + namecount * nameentrysize` (see\n    `pcre2_pattern_info.c` case PCRE2_INFO_NAMETABLE: the name table starts at\n    `re + sizeof(pcre2_real_code)`, and the code follows the name table — verify\n    this in `pcre2_intmodedep.h`/`pcre2_compile.c`).  Then call `find_bracket` with\n    number = 0, 1..n, n+1, and a NEGATIVE number (the \"find first OP_REVERSE /\n    OP_VREVERSE\" mode, which has no in-tree caller), for patterns containing\n    capture groups, named groups, XCLASS/ECLASS items, callout strings, \\p{..},\n    (*MARK:x), fixed and variable lookbehinds.  Compare the result as an OFFSET\n    from codestart (or \"NULL\"), never as an absolute pointer.\n11. `_pcre2_xclass_8(c, data, char_lists_end, utf)` and\n    `_pcre2_eclass_8(c, data_start, data_end, char_lists_end, utf)`: find out how\n    the in-tree callers build these arguments (grep `PRIV(xclass)(` and\n    `PRIV(eclass)(` in `pcre2_match.c`, `pcre2_dfa_match.c`, `pcre2_xclass.c`,\n    `pcre2_compile_class.c`, and see how `char_lists_end` is derived from the\n    compiled code — look for `char_lists_end` / `OP_XCLASS` / `OP_ECLASS` in\n    `pcre2_intmodedep.h` and `pcre2_match.c`).  Compile patterns whose FIRST opcode\n    is an XCLASS (e.g. `[\\x{100}-\\x{200}]`, `[^\\p{L}]`, a class with many ranges)\n    or an ECLASS (e.g. `(?[\\p{L}&&[a-z]])` — needs `PCRE2_ALT_EXTENDED_CLASS` for\n    the `(?[...])` syntax, check which option is required), locate the opcode at\n    `codestart`, and call the functions directly for many code points (0..0x2ff\n    exhaustively plus randoms up to 0x10ffff), utf TRUE and FALSE.\n    If, after reading the C, you conclude a direct call cannot be constructed\n    safely for one of these, say so explicitly in your report and instead compare\n    them INDIRECTLY by matching the same patterns against subjects that hit every\n    branch — but try hard to do the direct call first.\n\nQUALITY BAR\n* Assertions must compare the FULL observable result (return value, every output\n  parameter, every written byte), and the failure message must print the input and\n  both results.\n* If C and Rust DIVERGE, do NOT weaken or delete the test and do NOT edit `src/`.\n  Leave the test in place, `#[ignore]`d ONLY if it blocks other tests, and report the\n  divergence precisely (inputs + C result + Rust result) in your final message.\n* The file must compile with no warnings-as-errors problems and every non-ignored\n  test must pass or be reported as a genuine divergence.\n\nReport: the list of `#[test]` functions with pass/fail, the number of randomized\ninputs per group, any symbol you could not call directly (with the reason), and every\ndivergence found (exact inputs and both results)."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2523,"cache_read_input_tokens":211944,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2523,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aeab5d90-512d-4436-80fd-a67670a151c9","uuid":"8e5d36d7-b28c-4c48-8fcf-d43c6b8d6733","timestamp":"2026-08-14T21:50:16.675Z"}
